      ":question/problem": "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{3}y &=9 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{5}y &=\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 3 & \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 3 & \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{132}{5}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{5}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 33\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{3}y &=9 \\\\ y &=33,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=33$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+\\frac{1}{3}(33)=9\\,\u21d2\\,x=9-11=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=33.$",
